.Sh DESCRIPTION
A reader or writer is a backend context owned by the caller and a handle
that points to it.
Each constructor fills
.Fa *ctx
and returns the handle, which holds
.Fa ctx
and a copy of the backend's vtable.
The context, and any buffer given to the constructor, must outlive the
handle and must not move while it is in use.
Nothing allocates except the growable writer, which grows its vector through
the allocator it was given.
.Pp
The backends are
.Dq fs ,
a file descriptor on Linux or a
.Vt HANDLE
on Windows,
.Dq mem ,
a caller-owned memory region, and
.Dq growable ,
a
.Vt t_vec ,
.Dq tcp ,
a connected stream socket, and
.Dq udp ,
a datagram socket.
Sockets are created with
.Xr xft_net 3 .
.Ss Types
.Bd -literal -offset indent
typedef struct s_reader {
	t_any			reader_ctx;
	t_reader_vtable	vtable;	/* stream, discard, stream_all,
				   discard_all, read, read_all */
} t_reader;

typedef struct s_writer {
	t_any			writer_ctx;
	t_writer_vtable	vtable;	/* drain_all, drain_fd, flush */
} t_writer;

typedef struct s_reader_ctx_fs {
	t_buffer	buffer;	/* staging for stream and discard */
	t_fd		fd;
} t_reader_fs;

typedef struct s_reader_ctx_memory {
	t_buffer	buffer;	/* bytes not yet read */
} t_reader_ctx_memory;

typedef struct s_reader_ctx_growable {
	t_vec		vec;	/* bytes not yet read */
} t_reader_ctx_growable;

typedef struct s_writer_ctx_fs {
	t_buffer_with_capacity	buffer;	/* staged bytes */
	t_fd					fd;
} t_writer_fs;

typedef struct s_writer_ctx_memory {
	t_buffer	buffer;	/* room not yet written */
} t_writer_ctx_memory;

typedef struct s_writer_ctx_growable {
	t_allocator	allocator;
	t_vec		vec;	/* bytes written */
} t_writer_ctx_growable;

typedef struct s_reader_ctx_tcp {
	t_buffer	buf;	/* scratch for discard on Windows */
	t_fd		socket;
} t_reader_ctx_tcp;

typedef struct s_reader_ctx_udp {
	t_buffer	buf;	/* staging for stream and discard */
	t_fd		socket;
	t_ip_addr	ip;	/* sender of the last datagram */
} t_reader_ctx_udp;

typedef struct s_writer_ctx_tcp {
	t_buffer_with_capacity	buf;	/* staged bytes */
	t_fd					socket;
} t_writer_ctx_tcp;

typedef struct s_writer_ctx_udp {
	t_buffer_with_capacity	buf;	/* the next datagram */
	t_fd					socket;
	t_ip_addr				ip;	/* destination */
} t_writer_ctx_udp;
.Ed
.Pp
.Vt t_writer_vtable Ns .drain_fd
has the type
.Bd -literal -offset indent
t_result	(*)(t_writer *w, t_fd fd, t_size limit, t_size *size)
.Ed
.Pp
and moves up to
.Fa limit
bytes from the handle
.Fa fd
into the writer, storing the count in
.Pf * Fa size .
The fs and tcp readers stream through it; see
.Sx Writers .
.Pp
.Vt t_iovec
holds
.Fa mem
and
.Fa size
and is the element type of
.Fn xft_writev .
.Ss Readers
.Fn xft_reader_stream
moves up to
.Fa limit
bytes from
.Fa r
into
.Fa w
and stores the count in
.Pf * Fa size .
It stops at
.Fa limit
or at the end of the input, so a count below
.Fa limit
means the input is exhausted.
Into a mem writer, or a growable writer from a handle that reports a size,
it fails instead when the input ends first; see
.Sx Writers .
.Fn xft_reader_discard
consumes up to
.Fa limit
bytes the same way and drops them.
.Fn xft_reader_read
copies up to
.Fa dst Ns .size
bytes into
.Fa dst
and stores the count in
.Pf * Fa size .
On an fs or tcp reader it is a single read from the handle, so the count can
be below
.Fa dst Ns .size
while more input follows.
.Pp
.Fn xft_reader_stream_all ,
.Fn xft_reader_discard_all
and
.Fn xft_reader_read_all
fail unless exactly
.Fa limit ,
or
.Fa dst Ns .size ,
bytes were transferred.
Bytes transferred before the input ended are still delivered and consumed.
.Pp
.Fn xft_reader_stream
on an fs reader passes its handle to the writer's
.Fa drain_fd ,
which reads straight into the writer's own buffer or memory, so the bytes
are not staged twice.
.Fn xft_reader_discard
reads at most
.Fa buffer Ns .size
bytes into the staging buffer per call.
.Fn xft_reader_read
on an fs reader reads straight into
.Fa dst ,
and
.Fn xft_reader_read_all
repeats the read until
.Fa dst
is full.
A read that returns no bytes is the end of the input.
On Linux interrupted calls are retried; on Windows a broken pipe is the end
of the input.
.Pp
The tcp reader streams through the writer's
.Fa drain_fd
like the fs reader.
.Fn xft_reader_read
on it is a single receive.
.Fn xft_reader_read_all
receives with
.Dv MSG_WAITALL ,
so the kernel fills
.Fa dst
in one call unless a signal or the end of the stream cuts it short, and
receives again after a signal.
.Fn xft_reader_discard
on Linux receives with
.Dv MSG_TRUNC ,
which drops the bytes in the kernel without copying them; on Windows it
receives into
.Fa buffer .
The end of the stream is the peer closing its side.
.Pp
The udp reader handles exactly one datagram per call.
.Fn xft_reader_read
receives the next datagram into
.Fa dst
and drops whatever does not fit.
.Fn xft_reader_stream
receives one datagram of at most
.Fa limit
bytes into
.Fa buffer
and passes it to
.Fn xft_writer_drain_all .
.Fn xft_reader_discard
drops the next datagram and reports its length, capped at
.Fa limit ;
on Linux it receives with
.Dv MSG_TRUNC
and copies nothing, on Windows it receives into
.Fa buffer .
Each call stores the sender's address in
.Fa ctx Ns ->ip .
.Pp
The mem reader takes bytes from the front of
.Fa src
and advances
.Fa ctx Ns ->buffer
past them.
The growable reader takes bytes from the front of
.Fa vec
and moves the remaining bytes down, shrinking
.Fa ctx Ns ->vec.buf.size .
.Ss Writers
.Fn xft_writer_drain_all
writes all of
.Fa data
to
.Fa w .
.Fn xft_writer_flush
pushes out anything the writer has staged.
.Pp
The fs writer stages bytes in
.Fa buffer ,
whose size becomes the staging capacity.
Data that fits in the free space is only copied there.
Otherwise the staged bytes and
.Fa data
are written together, with one
.Fn xft_writev
call on Linux and one
.Ql WriteFile
call per piece on Windows, repeated until everything is written, and the
staging buffer is left empty.
.Fn xft_writer_flush
writes the staged bytes with
.Fn xft_write
or
.Ql WriteFile
until none are left.
Nothing is flushed implicitly; staged bytes are lost unless
.Fn xft_writer_flush
is called.
.Pp
The fs writer's
.Fa drain_fd
flushes the staged bytes, then repeatedly reads up to the staging capacity
from the handle into the staging buffer and flushes the previous chunk; the
last chunk stays staged until the next flush.
.Pp
The tcp writer stages bytes like the fs writer.
When the staged bytes and
.Fa data
do not fit, they are sent together, the staged bytes with
.Dv MSG_MORE
on Linux so that they share a segment with
.Fa data .
.Fn xft_writer_flush
sends the staged bytes.
Its
.Fa drain_fd
flushes, then on Linux moves the bytes with
.Xr sendfile 2 ,
without copying them through user space.
When the first
.Xr sendfile 2
call fails before anything was sent, as it does for a pipe, a terminal or a
socket, it falls back to reading into the staging buffer and sending each
chunk.
On Windows it always reads and sends chunks.
.Pp
The udp writer sends one datagram to
.Fa dest
per flush.
.Fn xft_writer_drain_all
appends to the staged bytes when
.Fa data
fits.
Otherwise the staged bytes go out as one datagram first, and
.Fa data
is then staged, or sent as a datagram of its own when it is larger than the
staging capacity.
.Fn xft_writer_flush
sends the staged bytes as one datagram and sends nothing when none are
staged.
Its
.Fa drain_fd
reads the handle in chunks of the staging capacity and sends each chunk as
one datagram; the last chunk stays staged until the next flush.
.Pp
The mem writer copies into
.Fa dst
and advances
.Fa ctx Ns ->buffer
past the bytes written, so the bytes written so far are
.Fa dst Ns .size
minus
.Fa ctx Ns ->buffer.size .
The growable writer appends to
.Fa ctx Ns ->vec
with
.Fn xft_vec_extend .
For both,
.Fn xft_writer_flush
does nothing.
The mem writer's
.Fa drain_fd
fills the smaller of
.Fa limit
and the room left with
.Fn xft_reader_read_all ,
reading straight into
.Fa dst ,
so it fails when the handle ends first.
The growable writer's
.Fa drain_fd
asks
.Fn xft_fileinfo_from_fd
for the size of the handle, reserves the smaller of that and
.Fa limit ,
and fills the reserved space with
.Fn xft_reader_read_all ,
so it fails when the handle ends first.
For a handle that reports a size of 0, such as a pipe, a terminal or a
socket, it reads into the spare capacity and grows the vector each
time it fills up.
The caller still owns the vector and frees it with
.Fn xft_vec_destroy
and
.Fa ctx Ns ->allocator .
.Ss Standard handles
.Fn xft_get_stdin ,
.Fn xft_get_stdout
and
.Fn xft_get_stderr
return the handles of the standard streams: 0, 1 and 2 on Linux, and the
results of
.Ql GetStdHandle
on Windows.
.Ss File information
.Fn xft_fileinfo_from_name
fills
.Pf * Fa fileinfo
for the file
.Fa name
without following a final symbolic link, so a link reports
.Dv F_T_SYML .
.Fn xft_fileinfo_from_fd
does the same for an open handle.
On Linux both use
.Xr statx 2 .
On Windows a handle that is not a disk file, such as a pipe or a console,
only gets its
.Fa type
filled in.
Times are nanoseconds since the Unix epoch.
.Vt t_file_info
is described in
.Xr xft_files 3 .
.Ss File mapping
.Fn xft_map_file_into_memory
maps the file
.Fa name
read-only and private and closes the descriptor before returning; the
mapping stays valid.
.Fn xft_unmap_file_from_memory
unmaps a buffer it returned.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
The reader and writer functions return
.Dv OK
on success and
.Dv KO
on failure.
An fs handle fails when a read or write fails or a write makes no progress.
A mem writer fails, without copying anything, when
.Fa data
is larger than the room left; its
.Fa drain_fd
fails when the handle ends before the smaller of
.Fa limit
and the room left is filled.
A growable writer fails when the vector cannot grow.
A tcp or udp handle fails when a send or receive fails; a udp writer also
fails when a datagram is sent short.
Mem and growable readers never fail.
.Pp
On Linux,
.Fa err
is the errno of the read, write, send or receive that failed.
A write or send that makes no progress, and a udp datagram sent short, give
.Dv xft_err_none .
A read that hits the end of the input before
.Fn xft_reader_read_all ,
.Fn xft_reader_stream_all
or
.Fn xft_reader_discard_all
has its full amount gives
.Dv xft_err_eof ,
and so does a mem writer's
.Fa drain_fd
that ends early.
A mem writer out of room gives
.Dv xft_err_nospace
and a growable writer that cannot grow
.Dv xft_err_oom .
Failures on Windows give
.Dv xft_err_none .
.Pp
The constructors return the handle and cannot fail.
.Pp
.Fn xft_fileinfo_from_name
and
.Fn xft_fileinfo_from_fd
return
.Dv OK ,
or
.Dv KO
if the file or handle cannot be queried, with the errno of
.Xr statx 2
in
.Fa err
on Linux and
.Dv xft_err_none
on Windows.
.Pp
.Fn xft_map_file_into_memory
returns the mapped bytes and the file size, or a zeroed
.Vt t_buffer
if opening, querying or mapping the file fails.
An empty file cannot be mapped and also yields a zeroed buffer.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Copy standard input to standard output:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
#include <xft/xft.h>

void	xft_main(const t_any *__restrict__ const sp)
{
	t_u8		inbuf[4096];
	t_u8		outbuf[4096];
	t_reader_fs	rctx;
	t_writer_fs	wctx;
	t_reader	r;
	t_writer	w;
	t_size		n;

	(void)sp;
	r = xft_fs_reader(&rctx, xft_get_stdin(),
			xft_fatptr(inbuf, sizeof(inbuf)));
	w = xft_fs_writer(&wctx, xft_get_stdout(),
			xft_fatptr(outbuf, sizeof(outbuf)));
	if (!xft_reader_stream(&r, &w, (t_size)-1, &n).ok)
		xft_exit_process(1);
	xft_exit_process(!xft_writer_flush(&w).ok);
}
.Ed
.Pp
Collect a reader's whole input in memory:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
t_result	slurp(t_allocator a, t_reader *r, t_writer_ctx_growable *ctx)
{
	t_vec		vec;
	t_writer	w;
	t_size		n;

	if (!xft_new_vec(a, &vec, 4096, 1).ok)
		return ((t_result){.ok = KO});
	w = xft_growable_writer(ctx, a, vec);
	if (!xft_reader_stream(r, &w, (t_size)-1, &n).ok)
		return (xft_vec_destroy(a, &ctx->vec), (t_result){.ok = KO});
	return ((t_result){.ok = OK});
}
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr xft_async 3 ,
.Xr xft_files 3 ,
.Xr xft_fmt 3 ,
